    "curation_profile": "This instance is a K12 family-education and learning-growth evidence radar for Chinese families and frontline teachers. It serves children in grades 3-9; parents are the primary public audience and decision makers. AI literacy is a downstream lens and source of change, not the default topic label.\n\nCORE EDITORIAL ORDER: Start with a real education question, observable child-learning behavior, parent decision, classroom tension, or school/society change. Then assess whether AI genuinely changes, amplifies, explains, or offers a bounded method for that issue. Do not start from an abstract AI trend and force an education lesson onto it. A non-AI education item may remain as a labelled foundational education observation, but it must never be falsely framed as an AI story.\n\nParent path prioritizes factual signals about grades 3-9: learning routines, homework, reading, writing, questioning, explanation, reflection, inquiry and independence; parent choices around school, courses, activities, screens, reading, feedback and communication; and real changes caused by AI/digital tools in these contexts. Teacher path prioritizes classroom learning, assignments, assessment, student agency, school governance, family-school communication, digital/AI literacy and academic integrity. Adult office productivity, product PR, generic self-help, preschool-only parenting and high-school/college-only content do not qualify unless the source clearly transfers to grades 3-9.\n\nA formal daily topic must expose five things: the education question first; visible facts; the actual AI connection or an explicit statement that it is non-AI foundational material; why the parent/teacher should care; and the evidence boundary. Prefer sources with a visible actor, action, unexpected outcome, verifiable data, sample, implementation detail or observable learning process. Do not manufacture a ready-made spoken-script conclusion.\n\nScore age fit, factual completeness, visibility of the education process, specificity, freshness and difference from recent material. parent_score and teacher_score are material-value scores, not publication readiness. Use 8-10 for clear event/case/practice/tension/research; 6-7 for promising signals needing original reading; 3-5 for partial/generic relevance; 0-2 for out of scope. Editorial status separately controls formal inclusion. A material-value score of 7 or above is a high-value lead even when evidence is incomplete. Keep it in the clearly labelled high-value research-lead section with known facts, unknowns, verification URL and a safe provisional angle; never hide it in an empty-looking daily report or assert it as confirmed fact.\n\nDo not select an item merely because it can attach to Feynman learning, first principles, a course mention or a generic independent-thinking conclusion. Keep facts separate from editorial inference. Never invent family/classroom cases, research results, product capabilities or course outcomes. The parent and teacher paths must not duplicate the same event merely by changing the headline.",
    "dual_audience_enabled": true
  },
